#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d15c03-982d-5609-ae43-1aa5a02e4e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283584} {:answer/id #uuid "5235ddc8-c95c-5d69-8fbb-4e14cf8db03d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283585} {:answer/id #uuid "a71f6336-91b3-5e54-a482-8aea120d420d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283586} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf21e88-dd9f-5184-a368-9e759f74739e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283587} {:answer/id #uuid "633ed4a5-ead4-563e-839d-486ec384fb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5235ddc8-c95c-5d69-8fbb-4e14cf8db03d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283585} :answer-field/id #uuid "a073bd65-687d-5e37-a1da-2b80521776eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a2a2113-9f29-565e-aae5-a6794706421f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104431" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-1$ and $det(A)=4-2k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=4-2k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =2-k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0bc6aa3-0324-5615-b408-ec5db1ead663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283591} {:answer/id #uuid "aae2140c-abab-5841-af79-8cb5f5c92f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283592} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea24c0c-a596-54af-b532-a3f976fc9ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283593} {:answer/id #uuid "b89d9b12-10de-5e84-8c9f-c9465a307bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283594} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5f6c56-c45f-5c45-ae15-a45910236188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b89d9b12-10de-5e84-8c9f-c9465a307bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283594} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6cccca-2231-5967-98d5-a3adff05ceef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd79571b-cfb2-59c8-988f-2f68aa4f8bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-18282" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3061cdb9-3f5e-578e-aa48-1b24ed90d2f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283598} {:answer/id #uuid "dd879f56-1774-57f5-bdfa-3ece34b0c42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283599} {:answer/id #uuid "3259e6e4-8f4a-5a93-a58a-3f5ccd7b0794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283600} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc7dec1-6220-541d-b15c-f7806182ca52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283601} {:answer/id #uuid "092a7670-38a2-597e-8b3c-c65a3e952468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd879f56-1774-57f5-bdfa-3ece34b0c42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283599} :answer-field/id #uuid "8233a48f-b290-5757-8915-04df2ffaa58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56f56a99-ae3f-56b5-9e3f-612f5a2d8c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-18285" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-0+(0-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15721863-0e64-5037-8661-654f92161b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283605} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3874f7-33ac-5785-95fd-5b1821b277e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15721863-0e64-5037-8661-654f92161b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283605} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02084d4-6c21-50e9-8635-0903f6bf1a7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0a5c074-ab5e-5f2d-8088-6f6048cd7aa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-319443" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}|\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(k-2)}\\cdot (k+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(k+2) & =-(k-2) \\\\ 2k+4 & =-k+2 \\\\ 3k & =-2 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b81913-a3e1-5578-afe1-bc783b842a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b81913-a3e1-5578-afe1-bc783b842a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283609} :answer-field/id #uuid "8775fdf4-6ce6-5e46-bc25-de993144ee74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29d3d120-8727-55cb-a11c-325a9dc83c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-319937" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot k)+(0\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)+(0\\cdot k-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2(k-2)+3-3 \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2k-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =-(k-2) \\\\ k-2 & =-1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
